1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,963
From the ground, night can make the world feel smaller.

2
00:00:06,123 --> 00:00:07,317
A window.

3
00:00:07,477 --> 00:00:08,681
A street.

4
00:00:08,841 --> 00:00:10,483
A circle of light.

5
00:00:10,883 --> 00:00:13,099
From orbit, the view changes.

6
00:00:13,259 --> 00:00:19,037
Darkness reveals a planet that is still moving, still active, and still connected.

7
00:00:19,437 --> 00:00:21,314
Look first at the surface.

8
00:00:21,474 --> 00:00:24,895
Clusters of light trace places where people live.

9
00:00:25,055 --> 00:00:27,444
Smaller points stretch between them.

10
00:00:27,604 --> 00:00:32,789
Coastlines and rivers emerge from patterns made by human activity.

11
00:00:32,949 --> 00:00:38,307
Satellite composites reveal these patterns across entire continents.

12
00:00:38,707 --> 00:00:41,885
These lights are only one layer of the scene.

13
00:00:42,045 --> 00:00:45,225
Above them, clouds move across the landscape.

14
00:00:45,385 --> 00:00:54,537
Brief flashes illuminate parts of the cloud cover: lightning, seen from a perspective most of us will never experience directly.

15
00:00:54,937 --> 00:00:57,475
Then the horizon begins to glow.

16
00:00:57,635 --> 00:01:04,334
A thin band follows the curve of Earth, separating the dark surface from the blackness beyond it.

17
00:01:04,734 --> 00:01:06,867
Some of that light is airglow.

18
00:01:07,027 --> 00:01:14,665
Atoms and molecules in the upper atmosphere release energy as light after being excited by sunlight.

19
00:01:14,825 --> 00:01:19,518
The night sky is not perfectly dark, even far from a city.

20
00:01:19,918 --> 00:01:24,398
From this perspective, the atmosphere becomes visible as a layer.

21
00:01:24,558 --> 00:01:27,630
Clouds occupy the lower part of the scene.

22
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:29,539
The glow sits higher.

23
00:01:29,699 --> 00:01:33,902
Stars appear beyond them, against the darkness of space.

24
00:01:34,302 --> 00:01:39,670
A bright object rising over the horizon adds another point of reference.

25
00:01:39,830 --> 00:01:43,222
In this astronaut sequence, it is the Moon.

26
00:01:43,382 --> 00:01:51,275
Its appearance changes with the camera's exposure, reminding us that a photograph records a view through an instrument.

27
00:01:51,675 --> 00:01:55,707
The motion in the astronaut recordings is accelerated.

28
00:01:55,867 --> 00:02:03,764
Individual photographs taken from the International Space Station have been combined into time-lapse sequences.

29
00:02:03,924 --> 00:02:06,783
Minutes of observation pass in seconds.

30
00:02:07,183 --> 00:02:09,636
That changes our sense of scale.

31
00:02:09,796 --> 00:02:11,993
A storm becomes a flicker.

32
00:02:12,153 --> 00:02:14,244
A city becomes a pattern.

33
00:02:14,404 --> 00:02:18,510
The atmosphere becomes a delicate boundary against space.

34
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:30,117
These images bring different kinds of light together: human activity below, weather in between, and the upper atmosphere responding to energy from the Sun.

35
00:02:30,517 --> 00:02:33,056
Night does not switch the planet off.

36
00:02:33,216 --> 00:02:35,755
It gives us another way to see it.
